WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.047 --> 00:00:04.880
ASHISH JHA: Why is
quality important to you,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.160
and how did you begin
working for this area?

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.590
MARGARET KRUK: First, I should
say I was a primary care

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.390
doc working in a rural,
remote northern Ontario,

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:18.290
and seeing lots of patients
in the hospital and my clinic,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:22.092
and really losing a lot of sleep over
how to give better care in a really

00:00:22.092 --> 00:00:23.300
resource constrained setting.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.700
For Canada, that was a
pretty difficult setting.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.150
And it was a tough job.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.170
And I think we struggled
on how to do that.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.190
And I think we did the best we
could in those circumstances, which

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.614
are still so much better than what most
of the world faces, of course, today.

00:00:39.614 --> 00:00:42.530
So I've been thinking about quality
as a professional for a long time.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.730
And then I think it came to
global health and public health

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.970
and trying to understand
people and patients,

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.984
and what are we seeing from the
population surveys, for example.

00:00:52.984 --> 00:00:55.400
It struck me, at the very
beginning of my research career,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.290
that people are using some
services but not others.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.750
And so I started asking why?

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:00.450
Why is this?

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.010
Why are you skipping facilities?

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:02.930
Why are you bypassing?

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.239
Why are you using this
facility for antenatal care,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:07.030
but you would never
come back for delivery?

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:07.940
What's going on with that?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:08.648
ASHISH JHA: Yeah.

00:01:08.648 --> 00:01:12.470
MARGARET KRUK: And that's actually
when the first signs of the importance

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.570
of quality emerged from our research.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.610
And the more work I
did with my colleagues

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.350
in Africa, the more people we asked,
the more experiments we performed,

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:24.140
and the more I read, the
more I realized that quality

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.620
is fundamental to people
in every part of the world

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.240
and for every health condition.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.010
Yes, they define it a bit differently.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.436
People who have a sick
child maybe are immediately

00:01:34.436 --> 00:01:37.310
focused on the competence of the
provider, whereas women for delivery

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.260
are more focused on
the interpersonal care.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.340
But they all want to get better.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.870
They all want strong skills and respect.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.640
So it's that body of
evidence and work that

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.910
really shifted my thinking to
asking the question, what's

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.620
actually going on at facilities then?

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.312
People demanded, their
actions are based on quality.

00:02:00.312 --> 00:02:02.270
What's actually happening
in the health system?

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.510
And so for the last
several years, our team

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.970
have been looking at just that question.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.020
And that's also led to this work on
the Lancet Global Health Commission

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.330
to try to interrogate the point of
intersection between systems and users,

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.250
because that's where
health care happens.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:22.400
And that's where health is built, at
least in terms of the health systems

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.930
contribution to population health.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.710
That's where all the work is done.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.950
So understanding what
people expectations are

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.380
and understanding what
it is they actually get

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.150
has been a huge preoccupation for me.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:38.120
I think if we don't attend to
that, then the health system is--

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.560
I think we forego a massive opportunity
to improve population health,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.630
because the health system
should take its place

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.171
as one of the determinants of health.

00:02:46.171 --> 00:02:47.530
ASHISH JHA: Yes.

